{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01XEvEvWtE7rzL1Bxquw81zs","type":"tool_result","content":"58\t// denormals, +/-inf, +/-0, zero-magnitude (division-by-zero) inputs,\n59\t// aliased and partially-overlapping pointers, negative lengths, and sizes up\n60\t// to 65536. All byte-identical.\n61\t//\n62\t// One caveat, inherent to the C rather than to this translation: the NaN\n63\t// *payload* that `spectral_contrast` returns is not fixed by the C source. It\n64\t// depends on the compiler's choice of SSE destination operand, which varies by\n65\t// compiler and optimization level -- eleven GCC/Clang configurations of the\n66\t// same C sources fall into three mutually disagreeing groups. This translation\n67\t// reproduces the largest group, which is also the one produced by every\n68\t// optimized GCC build (`-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3`, `-Os`, and\n69\t// `cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`) plus `clang -O1`; against those the match\n70\t// is exact, with a zero-line diff.\n71\t//\n72\t// Everything the C language actually specifies is bit-exact under all eleven\n73\t// configurations. In particular `match`'s integer return value never differed\n74\t// once across the entire corpus in any configuration, and neither did\n75\t// `spectral_contrast` on non-NaN data.\n76\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n77\t\n78\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n79\t\n80\t/// `#define N_SMOOTH 16` from match.h -- size of the smoothing kernel.\n81\tconst N_SMOOTH: c_int = 16;\n82\t\n83\t// ===========================================================================\n84\t// Bit-exact scalar SSE arithmetic\n85\t// ===========================================================================\n86\t//\n87\t// For every finite/infinite input these helpers are plain IEEE-754 operations."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ce1be1d-a5a4-493e-9931-fa82fd487ae7","uuid":"8aebea9b-d31b-44a7-a2a2-0251023d410c","timestamp":"2026